    {
      "content": "<h3>How HashSet works inside</h3>\n<p>A HashSet is a thin wrapper over a <code>HashMap&lt;E, Object&gt;</code>; every element is a key and every value is the same dummy object <code>PRESENT</code>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Step 1, hash:</strong> <code>h = e.hashCode()</code>, then spread: <code>h ^ (h &gt;&gt;&gt; 16)</code> so the high bits also influence the low bits.</li>\n<li><strong>Step 2, bucket:</strong> the table length is always a power of two, so the index is <code>(n - 1) &amp; hash</code>, a fast \"modulo\".</li>\n<li><strong>Step 3, compare:</strong> inside the bucket Java compares hash codes first, then <code>==</code> / <code>equals()</code>. A match means \"already present\".</li>\n<li><strong>Collisions:</strong> elements that land in the same bucket form a linked list; if a bucket reaches <strong>8</strong> entries and the table has at least <strong>64</strong> buckets, the list is converted to a balanced <strong>red-black tree</strong> (O(log n) instead of O(n)).</li>\n<li><strong>Resize:</strong> when <code>size &gt; capacity * loadFactor</code> (default 16 x 0.75 = 12) the table <strong>doubles</strong> and every entry is redistributed.</li>\n<li><strong>Capacity from the copy constructor:</strong> <code>max(size / 0.75 + 1, 16)</code>, so copying avoids early resizes.</li>\n<li>Because the order depends on hash codes and table size, iteration order can change after a resize.</li>\n</ul>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Trap: equals() and hashCode() must agree</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Rule: <strong>equal objects must have equal hash codes</strong>.</li>\n<li>If a class overrides <code>equals</code> but not <code>hashCode</code>, two equal objects usually land in different buckets, so the set keeps both and <code>contains</code> fails.</li>\n<li>A <code>record</code> (or an IDE-generated pair) gets both right.</li>\n</ul>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// HashSet with your own class: equals() AND hashCode() are required\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.Objects;\nimport java.util.Set;\n\npublic class HashSetEqualsHashCodeDemo {\n    static class BadFort {                              // equals() but NO hashCode()\n        final String name;\n        BadFort(String name) { this.name = name; }\n        @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof BadFort b && b.name.equals(name); }\n    }\n\n    static class GoodFort {                             // both methods\n        final String name;\n        GoodFort(String name) { this.name = name; }\n        @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof GoodFort g && g.name.equals(name); }\n        @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(name); }\n    }\n\n    record RecordFort(String name) { }                  // generated equals() and hashCode()\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<BadFort> bad = new HashSet<>();\n        bad.add(new BadFort(\"Raigad\"));\n        bad.add(new BadFort(\"Raigad\"));\n        System.out.println(\"Missing hashCode -> size \" + bad.size() + \", contains? \" + bad.contains(new BadFort(\"Raigad\")));\n\n        Set<GoodFort> good = new HashSet<>();\n        good.add(new GoodFort(\"Raigad\"));\n        good.add(new GoodFort(\"Raigad\"));\n        System.out.println(\"Both methods     -> size \" + good.size() + \", contains? \" + good.contains(new GoodFort(\"Raigad\")));\n\n        Set<RecordFort> records = new HashSet<>();\n        records.add(new RecordFort(\"Raigad\"));\n        records.add(new RecordFort(\"Raigad\"));\n        System.out.println(\"Record           -> size \" + records.size());\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Missing hashCode -> size 2, contains? false\n// Both methods     -> size 1, contains? true\n// Record           -> size 1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Trap: changing an element after adding it</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The bucket of an element is chosen from its <code>hashCode</code> <strong>when it is added</strong>.</li>\n<li>If you later change a field that <code>hashCode</code> uses, the element sits in the wrong bucket: <code>contains</code> and <code>remove</code> can no longer find it.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>immutable</strong> classes (records, Strings, Integers) as set elements.</li>\n</ul>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// A mutable element changes its hash code and gets \"lost\" in the set\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.Objects;\nimport java.util.Set;\n\npublic class HashSetMutableTrapDemo {\n    static class Fort {\n        String name;                                       // NOT final: can change\n        Fort(String name) { this.name = name; }\n        @Override public boolean equals(Object o) { return o instanceof Fort f && f.name.equals(name); }\n        @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(name); }\n        @Override public String toString() { return name; }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<Fort> forts = new HashSet<>();\n        Fort raigad = new Fort(\"Raigad\");\n        forts.add(raigad);\n\n        raigad.name = \"Rajgad\";                            // the hash code changes now\n\n        System.out.println(\"Set shows it       : \" + forts);\n        System.out.println(\"contains(raigad)   : \" + forts.contains(raigad));          // looks in the wrong bucket\n        System.out.println(\"remove(raigad)     : \" + forts.remove(raigad));            // cannot remove it either\n        System.out.println(\"size afterwards    : \" + forts.size());\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Set shows it       : [Rajgad]\n// contains(raigad)   : false\n// remove(raigad)     : false\n// size afterwards    : 1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Choosing a Set implementation</h3>\n<table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"8\" cellspacing=\"0\" style=\"border-collapse:collapse; width:100%;\">\n<tr><th style=\"text-align:left;\"></th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">HashSet</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">LinkedHashSet</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">TreeSet</th><th style=\"text-align:left;\">CopyOnWriteArraySet</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Order</td><td>None</td><td>Insertion order</td><td>Sorted (natural or Comparator)</td><td>Insertion order</td></tr>\n<tr><td>add / remove / contains</td><td><strong>O(1)</strong> average</td><td>O(1) average</td><td>O(log n)</td><td>O(n)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inside</td><td>HashMap</td><td>HashMap + linked list</td><td>Red-black tree (TreeMap)</td><td>Array copied on write</td></tr>\n<tr><td>null</td><td>One allowed</td><td>One allowed</td><td>Not allowed (natural order)</td><td>Allowed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thread-safe</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use for</td><td><strong>Default set</strong></td><td>Predictable iteration</td><td>Sorted data, range queries</td><td>Few writes, many readers</td></tr>\n</table>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Best practices</h3>\n<p>Habits for fast and correct sets.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Declare with the interface: <code>Set&lt;String&gt; forts = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();</code>.</li>\n<li>Give an expected size to avoid resizes: <code>new HashSet&lt;&gt;((int) (expected / 0.75f) + 1)</code> or <code>HashSet.newHashSet(expected)</code> (Java 19+).</li>\n<li>Use immutable element types; always override <code>equals</code> and <code>hashCode</code> together (or use a <code>record</code>).</li>\n<li>Do not depend on iteration order; use <code>LinkedHashSet</code> or <code>TreeSet</code> when you need one.</li>\n<li>Prefer a <code>Set</code> over a <code>List</code> whenever you mostly ask \"is it in there?\" or must keep values unique.</li>\n<li>To share between threads use <code>ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()</code> or <code>Collections.synchronizedSet</code>.</li>\n</ul>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Real-time practice 1: Visitor registration: no duplicates, and set arithmetic</h3>\n<p>Two forts keep a set of registered visitor IDs. The office must reject double registration and answer questions such as \"who visited both forts?\"</p>\n<p><strong>What the program does:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>add</strong> returns false for a duplicate: use it to detect double registration.</li>\n<li><strong>retainAll</strong> = visited both (intersection), <strong>addAll</strong> = visited either (union), <strong>removeAll</strong> = only one fort (difference).</li>\n<li>Always copy first so the original sets stay intact.</li>\n<li><strong>contains</strong> for an O(1) gate check.</li>\n</ol>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// Real-time practice: registrations and set arithmetic with HashSet\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class VisitorRegistry {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set<String> raigad = new HashSet<>();\n        for (String id : new String[] {\"PUN-101\", \"MUM-202\", \"PUN-101\", \"NSK-303\", \"NGP-404\"}) {\n            if (!raigad.add(id)) {\n                System.out.println(\"Rejected duplicate registration: \" + id);\n            }\n        }\n\n        Set<String> torna = new HashSet<>(Set.of(\"MUM-202\", \"KOP-505\", \"NGP-404\", \"SAT-606\"));\n\n        Set<String> both = new HashSet<>(raigad);\n        both.retainAll(torna);                                   // intersection\n        Set<String> either = new HashSet<>(raigad);\n        either.addAll(torna);                                    // union\n        Set<String> onlyRaigad = new HashSet<>(raigad);\n        onlyRaigad.removeAll(torna);                             // difference\n\n        System.out.println(\"Visited both forts   : \" + new TreeSet<>(both));\n        System.out.println(\"Visited either fort  : \" + new TreeSet<>(either));\n        System.out.println(\"Visited only Raigad  : \" + new TreeSet<>(onlyRaigad));\n        System.out.println(\"Gate check PUN-101   : \" + raigad.contains(\"PUN-101\"));\n        System.out.println(\"Raigad is subset of union: \" + either.containsAll(raigad));\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Rejected duplicate registration: PUN-101\n// Visited both forts   : [MUM-202, NGP-404]\n// Visited either fort  : [KOP-505, MUM-202, NGP-404, NSK-303, PUN-101, SAT-606]\n// Visited only Raigad  : [NSK-303, PUN-101]\n// Gate check PUN-101   : true\n// Raigad is subset of union: true"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Real example: tracking unique visitors of each fort</h3>\n<p>A visitor log with repeated entries. A set per fort counts each visitor once, and the intersection finds visitors who came to both.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// Unique visitors per fort, and visitors who went to both\nimport java.util.Arrays;\nimport java.util.HashSet;\nimport java.util.Set;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class FortVisitorsDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String[] raigadLog = {\"Pune\", \"Nashik\", \"Pune\", \"Nagpur\", \"Pune\"};      // arrival city per entry\n        String[] tornaLog = {\"Nagpur\", \"Indore\", \"Nagpur\", \"Pune\"};\n\n        Set<String> raigad = new HashSet<>();\n        for (String city : raigadLog) {\n            raigad.add(city);                          // repeated cities count once\n        }\n        Set<String> torna = new HashSet<>(Arrays.asList(tornaLog));   // the copy constructor accepts duplicates\n        System.out.println(\"Raigad entries \" + raigadLog.length + \" -> unique cities \" + raigad.size());\n        System.out.println(\"Torna entries  \" + tornaLog.length + \" -> unique cities \" + torna.size());\n\n        Set<String> both = new TreeSet<>(raigad);\n        both.retainAll(torna);\n        System.out.println(\"Cities that visited both: \" + both);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Raigad entries 5 -> unique cities 3\n// Torna entries  4 -> unique cities 3\n// Cities that visited both: [Nagpur, Pune]"
      ]
    }
